## Search over explanations and compatible products

### What you will learn

Treat a hypothesis or a product configuration as the chosen object.

### Choose a feature that explains the labels

This toy learner chooses one feature. Every positive example must have it and every negative example must lack it. Only stripes separates the two examples. Larger decision trees and rule-learning projects search a richer hypothesis space.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    feature(stripes;round).
    positive(sample1). negative(sample2).
    has(sample1,stripes). has(sample1,round). has(sample2,round).
    1 { hypothesis(Feature) : feature(Feature) } 1.
    :- hypothesis(Feature), positive(Example), not has(Example,Feature).
    :- hypothesis(Feature), negative(Example), has(Example,Feature).
    #show hypothesis/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["hypothesis(stripes)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Generate a product and enforce compatibility

Choose one frame and one motor. The compact frame cannot use the heavy motor, so three products remain. Unlike the hypothesis example, this model checks compatibility of chosen components rather than agreement with training labels.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    frame(compact;cargo). motor(light;heavy).
    1 { fitted_frame(Frame) : frame(Frame) } 1.
    1 { fitted_motor(Motor) : motor(Motor) } 1.
    :- fitted_frame(compact), fitted_motor(heavy).
    #show fitted_frame/1. #show fitted_motor/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

3 = length(result.models)
true = Enum.all?(result.models, &(length(&1.shown) == 2))
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Add has(sample2,stripes). to the learner. Separately remove the incompatibility constraint from configuration.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

No one-feature classifier now separates the examples. With no component incompatibility, the product model has four configurations.

</details>

## Learn a propositional classifier

Exercise files: [instance-06-03.lp](../assets/propositional/instances/instance-06-03.lp) · [propositional.lp](../assets/propositional/propositional.lp) · [instance-06-03.json](../assets/propositional/solutions/instance-06-03.json).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/propositional", __DIR__))
```

Construct propositional formulas over the supplied Boolean attributes and evaluate them on every labelled example. Use exactly the requested formula size, counting attributes and connectives as nodes. Maximize correctly classified examples, and preserve the supplied connective-order symmetry restrictions.

Complete the starter encoding before comparing its projected models with the supplied solution data.

### Keep the data contract

The instance supplies `attribute/1`, `example/2`, `has/2` and `size`. Read the template for the formula-node representation and preserve its show signature. Start by evaluating one hand-written formula before searching over formulas.

