## Learn an acyclic rule system

Exercise files: [acyclic-programs.lp](../assets/acyclic-programs/acyclic-programs.lp) · [instance-006-4-2-0.lp](../assets/acyclic-programs/instances/instance-006-4-2-0.lp) · [instance-006-4-2-0.json](../assets/acyclic-programs/solutions/instance-006-4-2-0.json).

```elixir
workdir =
  Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/acyclic-programs", __DIR__))
```

Learn a bounded normal program that separates positive and negative training examples. Its atom-dependency graph must have no cycle, including dependencies through negated body atoms. Auxiliary atom 1 is the positive-class output. Classify every training example correctly. Among correct encodings, minimize the number of rules first and then the total number of body literals.

Complete the starter encoding before comparing its projected models with the supplied solution data.

### Keep the data contract

Inputs are `attribute/1`, `example/2`, `has/2` and the program/body size bounds. Outputs use `head/2` and `body/3`, with signs 1 and -1. Rule IDs and auxiliary IDs must be contiguous; preserve the supplied symmetry convention.

### Develop and check the encoding

Complete the editable encoding below. Compare its shown atom sets with the JSON answers in `solutions`; `optimal_cost` records a proven optimum when present. Download your edits before cleanup.

```elixir
~S"""
#show head/2.
#show body/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-006-4-2-0.lp")])
|> Course.display()
```

### Check boundary cases

Test the smallest instance first, then check each derived relation. Compare projected model sets and, when optimizing, proven costs.

