## Read a COOM product description

COOM describes a product domain before translation into ASP. Read a description in three layers: its component hierarchy, each component’s feature domains, and the restrictions relating feature values.

### Components and features

A product names the root of the hierarchy. Structures describe reusable kinds of component. Fields attach child components with lower and upper cardinalities; enumerations give finite alternatives for feature values. Attributes belong to a particular component context, so the same feature name on two component types need not refer to the same value.

### Restrictions and derived values

Combination tables specify allowed or forbidden tuples. A requirement rules out assignments that fail a relation. Conditions make a restriction applicable only in its stated context. Defaults supply proposed values; distinguish these from mandatory requirements. Calculations derive numerical values from other features. Parent/context references allow a component to refer to related components without treating their attributes as global names.

### Inspect before translating

Read the example files in [the COOM instances](../assets/configuration/instances), then compare [their generated ASP facts](../assets/configuration/instances). The [fact vocabulary](03-learning-and-configuration.livemd#understand-configuration-facts) explains the encoding boundary. The Livebooks solve those facts with native clingo, but do not execute the COOM parser. Preserve its actual input syntax when extending a product description; this summary does not define a replacement DSL.

## Understand configuration facts

The parser output describes a configuration problem as data rather than solving it. `structure/1` declares component kinds; the included generated bike facts use `"ROOT"` as their root context. `feature/5` records a feature’s context, name, type and minimum/maximum cardinality. Enumeration and attribute facts specify legal values and their component context.

A constraint’s representation records both its operands and the relation between them. Keep operand paths relative to their specified context; a feature name alone can be ambiguous. Bounds belong to the declaration they accompany and must not be treated as a global count.

Use [the generated ASP instances](../assets/configuration/instances) as concrete input-format references. In the basic bike instance each wheel occurs exactly once, both draw from the same finite type domain, and the equality requirement forces their values to agree. The solver’s `value/2` atoms represent a completed assignment; the declaration facts themselves are not a solution.

When extending the encoding, first support the constructs used by the smallest instance, then add each extra construct exercised by the larger examples. Check both missing-value and incompatible-value cases. This lesson uses the supplied generated facts and does not implement a COOM parser.

## Configure a product from compatible parts

Exercise files: [configuration.lp](../assets/configuration/configuration.lp) · [bike-1-basic.lp](../assets/configuration/instances/bike-1-basic.lp) · [bike-2-extended.lp](../assets/configuration/instances/bike-2-extended.lp) · [bike-1-basic.json](../assets/configuration/solutions/bike-1-basic.json) · [bike-2-extended.json](../assets/configuration/solutions/bike-2-extended.json) · [bike-1-basic.coom](../assets/configuration/instances/bike-1-basic.coom).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/configuration", __DIR__))
```

A product configuration selects components and feature values while satisfying compatibility constraints. A partially specified configuration should be extendable to a complete one, or produce an explanation of the conflict. The small bike instance has two wheels whose types must agree; its four permitted wheel types give four complete configurations.

The cells inspect the COOM description, generated ASP facts and basic configuration rules. Native clingo solves the ASP. The generated ASP facts are the solver input; this lesson does not implement a COOM parser or a product UI.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "instances/bike-1-basic.coom") |> IO.puts()
```

### Extend the configuration encoding

Use the instances in `instances` to extend `configuration.lp`. The output contract is `value/2`. Make the rules reusable across products, rather than hard-coding the bike example.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "configuration.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    files: [
      Path.join(workdir, "configuration.lp"),
      Path.join(workdir, "instances/bike-1-basic.lp")
    ],
    models: :all
  )

expected = Course.read(workdir, "solutions/bike-1-basic.json") |> JSON.decode!()
true = result.exhausted
true = Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, &Enum.sort(&1.shown))) == expected["models"]
Course.display(result)
```

### Design the interaction

Extend the domain encoding for a product of your choice. Use assumptions or external inputs for selections, reject incompatible combinations, and define how to explain a conflict. Keep UI design outside the solver encoding.

### Test complete configurations

The basic instance above checks its complete projected model set against the JSON answers. Use the same comparison for the extended instance after completing the encoding. Preserve the chosen cardinality convention. A blank or partial template may be satisfiable without representing a valid configuration.

```elixir
Course.archive(workdir, "configuration.zip")
```

Download edited files before workspace cleanup. The extended starter below returns eight configurations because it ignores the `combinations/3` and `allow/3` compatibility table. Implement that table: support must be `True` for W14 or W16 and `False` for W18 or W20. The expected answers contain the four valid configurations.

```elixir
~S"""
{ value(F,V) : option(T,V) } = 1 :- feature(_,F,T,_,_).
:- behavior((S,C)), require((S,C),B), not holds_binary(B).
holds_binary(B) :- binary(_,B,Left,"=",Right), value(Left,LV), value(Right,RV), LV = RV.
#show value/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/bike-2-extended.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

